    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "\\[g(f(x))=3x+4 \\Longrightarrow (2x-5)(ax+b)=3x+4.\\]\nExpanding the left side, we obtain\n\\[2ax^2+(2b-5a)x-5b=3x+4,\\]\nwhich implies\n\\[2ax^2+(2b-5a-3)x-5b-4=0.\\]\nSince the coefficients on both sides of the equation must be equal for all $x$, this implies that all the coefficients on the left side must be equal to the corresponding coefficients on the right side. Therefore, we have\n\\begin{align*}\na &= 0, \\\\\n2b-5a-3 &= 0, \\\\\n-5b-4 &= 0.\n\\end{align*}From the third equation, we find $b=-\\frac{4}{5}$. Plugging this into the second equation, we find $a=0$. Thus,\n\\[a+b=0-\\frac{4}{5}=\\boxed{-\\frac{4}{5}}.\\]",
