    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "We expand $g(f(x)) = g(ax+b)$ to get $3x+4$.\n\nFirst, we find $f(x)$, and we get $f(x) = ax+b$.\n\nNext, we find $g(f(x))$, and we get $g(ax+b) = 2(ax+b) - 5$.\n\nContinuing, we expand $g(ax+b)$ into $(2a)x + (2b - 5)$.\n\nFinally, we set $(2a)x + (2b - 5) = 3x + 4$. Because this is an equality for all $x$, we get:\n\n\\[2a=3 \\Rightarrow a=\\boxed{\\frac{3}{2}}\\]\n\\[2b - 5 = 4 \\Rightarrow a + b = \\frac{3}{2} + 4 = \\boxed{\\frac{11}{2}}\\] The answer is: \\frac{11}{2}",
