    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) given that \\(g(f(x)) = 3x + 4\\). We start by expressing \\(g(f(x))\\) in terms of \\(x\\) using the given functions \\(f(x)\\) and \\(g(x)\\).\n\nFirst, we find \\(f(x)\\). The function \\(f(x)\\) is given by:\n\\[ f(x) = ax + b. \\]\n\nNext, we substitute \\(f(x)\\) into \\(g(x)\\):\n\\[ g(f(x)) = g(ax + b) = 2(ax + b) - 5. \\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[ g(f(x)) = 2ax + 2b - 5. \\]\n\nWe are given that for all \\(x\\), \\(g(f(x)) = 3x + 4\\). Therefore, we can set the expressions for \\(g(f(x))\\) equal to each other:\n\\[ 2ax + 2b - 5 = 3x + 4. \\]\n\nFor these two expressions to be equal for all \\(x\\), the coefficients of \\(x\\) and the constant terms must be equal. This gives us the following system of equations:\n\\[ 2a = 3, \\]\n\\[ 2b - 5 = 4. \\]\n\nWe solve the first equation for \\(a\\):\n\\[ 2a = 3 \\implies a = \\frac{3}{2}. \\]\n\nWe solve the second equation for \\(b\\):\n\\[ 2b - 5 = 4 \\implies 2b = 9 \\implies b = \\frac{9}{2}. \\]\n\nNow, we need to find \\(a + b\\):\n\\[ a + b = \\frac{3}{2} + \\frac{9}{2} = \\frac{3 + 9}{2} = \\frac{12}{2} = 6. \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(a + b\\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{6}. \\]",
